# Hilavitkutin: Architecture Design

> Morsel-driven pipeline execution engine. `#![no_std]`, no alloc.
> Consumers declare WorkUnits with typed read/write access sets; the
> engine analyses them into phases/trunks/fibers, compiles per-core
> dispatch programs, runs them on a pre-allocated thread pool.



## Crate layout

| Crate | Role |
|-------|------|
| `hilavitkutin-api` | Consumer-facing types + traits + platform contracts. WorkUnit, AccessSet, Column/Resource/Virtual, Context, Contains, Clock/MemoryProvider/ThreadPool. Consumer contract surface (capability / sink / codec) for API positions that receive or emit items outside WU execution. |
| `hilavitkutin-api-macros` | Proc-macro companion to `hilavitkutin-api`. Emits `#[derive(ResourceFootprint)]`, which sums a resource value type's `Field`/`Seq`/`Map` field footprints so the engine reads its L1-morsel write-collection budget (canonical R5). |
| `hilavitkutin` | Engine: plan, dispatch, resource, thread, strategy, adapt, scheduler. |
| `hilavitkutin-build` | Build-time optimisations (LLVM passes, MIR, cfg emission, PGO/BOLT workflows). Standalone build-dep, no runtime deps. |
| `hilavitkutin-contracts` | The contracts a kernel author and a store implementor write against: domains, columns, resources and read-list members, the value kernel `ValueKernel` with `KernelId`, the bind with the checked table `Bound`, which holds its store and cuts its staged side one part per unit, and the typed handle `Col`, the store with its visiting order, the digest, and the number proxies. Depends on notko and notko-hlist only. |
| `hilavitkutin-ctx` | Provider-gated context framework (ecosystem, standalone). |
| `hilavitkutin-extensions` | Contract-bound extension host orchestration (descriptor, lifecycle, capability dispatch, failure policy) layered over `hilavitkutin-linking`. Domain-agnostic. `no_std`, no allocator, no dyn. |
| `hilavitkutin-extensions-macros` | Proc-macro companion to `hilavitkutin-extensions`. Emits `#[repr(C)]` descriptor + exported-symbol fn + capability trampolines via `#[export_extension]`. One of two proc-macro crates in the stack (with `hilavitkutin-api-macros`). |
| `hilavitkutin-frozen-authors` | Frozen kernels, each beside its model, written against `hilavitkutin-contracts` alone and held under a ledger the `frozen-ledger` lint checks. Author 1 is `Diffuse` on the two-axis domain `Plane`. |
| `hilavitkutin-frozen-implementors` | Frozen stores and visiting orders, written against `hilavitkutin-contracts` alone and held under a ledger the `frozen-ledger` lint checks: `Columns` and `Records`, `Forward` and `Permuted`. |
| `hilavitkutin-kit` | Kit preset trait. Declarative bundle: `type Units` (cons-list of WorkUnits) plus `type Owned` (cons-list of Resources / Columns / Virtuals). Engine reads associated types at compile time on `.add_kit::<K>()`. Standalone (no other hilavitkutin-crate deps). |
| `hilavitkutin-laws` | The laws a runner and a store are held to, as functions over plain data returning a `Verdict`; a law comparing runs refuses fewer than two. Depends on the contracts and on no runner. |
| `hilavitkutin-linking` | Cross-platform dynamic library loading primitive (dlopen / LoadLibrary + typed Symbol / StaticRef). Pull-based explicit-symbol resolution. `no_std`, no allocator. Renamed from prior `hilavitkutin-extensions`. |
| `hilavitkutin-persistence` | Generic hot-cold storage bridge (ecosystem, standalone). |
| `hilavitkutin-providers` | Default Resource-backed providers for the scheduler. v0 ships the interner value-type surface (InternerApi / HasInterner / MemoryArena + default_interner constructor) plus the declarative `InternerKit` (round-4 shape: `type Units = read![]`, `type Owned = read![Resource<...>]`). Future modules add ColumnStorage / Clock / Memory defaults per consumer demand. Standalone (no engine dep). |
| `hilavitkutin-reference` | The one-thread reference runner, kept as the oracle every other runner is held to: `step`, `digest` and `run` over a checked table, which holds its store and cuts each unit its staged part. |
| `hilavitkutin-sym` | Generic interned-identity core: the `Sym` handle, domain traits, `Interner`, `Generator` (ecosystem, standalone). `hilavitkutin-str` builds on it. |
| `hilavitkutin-str` | Interned string system, one domain of `hilavitkutin-sym` (ecosystem, standalone). |

## Dependency graph

A consumer crate depends on `hilavitkutin`. The engine pulls `hilavitkutin-api` plus the arvo crates it needs at runtime: `arvo`, `arvo-bitmask`, `arvo-graph`, `arvo-sparse`, `arvo-spectral` (which itself depends on `arvo-sparse`), and `arvo-comb` (which depends on `arvo-bitmask`). A consumer's `build.rs` separately depends on `hilavitkutin-build`, which has no runtime deps.

hilavitkutin-ctx, -persistence, -sym, -str, -kit, -providers are standalone ecosystem crates that consumers opt into. ctx, persistence, sym, str are pure ecosystem (engine does not depend on them). hilavitkutin-str depends on hilavitkutin-sym for the identity handle; hilavitkutin-sym itself depends only on the foundations (arvo, notko), sitting below the rest of the ecosystem. kit is a contract crate the engine depends on for its `.add_kit()` method, but it has no reverse dependency on the engine; consumers can author Kits without pulling the engine in. providers ships default Kit impls consumers wire in (interner, future ColumnStorage / Clock / Memory defaults); it consumes api + str + kit + the foundations crates, no reverse dep.

hilavitkutin-linking, hilavitkutin-extensions, hilavitkutin-extensions-macros make up the plugin-host layer. Downstream consumer ecosystems build domain-specific contract crates on top.

hilavitkutin-contracts, -reference, -laws, -frozen-authors and -frozen-implementors make up the loop core, and none of them depends on `hilavitkutin` or `hilavitkutin-api`. The contracts depend on notko and notko-hlist only; the frozen crates depend on the contracts only; the laws depend on the contracts and on no runner; the reference runner depends on the contracts. `hilavitkutin-api` is the legacy contract until the engine is ported onto the contracts, and is then removed.

## Runtime

The runtime layer (Scheduler + Executor + ExecutionPlan + dispatch + morsel loop + adapt + persistence wiring) crystallised in design round 202605101036. The locked shape:

### `Scheduler::run()` signature

Parameterless `run(&mut self) -> notko::Outcome<Cfg::Ok, Cfg::Err>`. `RunCfg` is consumer-supplied via `.with(my_cfg)` builder method; defaults provided via `HasTrivialCtor`. Dispatch routing happens at the type level via `RunCfgDispatch<Self>` slot. `Scheduler<'frame, Cfg, E>` propagates a frame lifetime through `ExecutionPlan<'frame, ...>` to the per-worker entry, validated by the PoolFrame lifetime sketch under nightly Rust.

Plan recompute fires on entry to `run()` when the dirty bit is set; otherwise the cached plan dispatches directly. The dirty bit is keyed per `PlanAffecting`-bound Resource.

### Plan algorithm chain (12 steps + step 13 FiberShape classification)

Free-function composition over the 12-step canonical chain inherited from the polka-dots prior art: `build_dag → topo_sort → upward_rank → waist → RCM → block-diag → spectral → fiber group → morsel size → phase config → column classify → dirty masks`. Round 202605101036 adds step 13 `shape_classification` producing the typed `FiberShape` projection consumed by dispatch codegen. The chain's output is a flat `ExecutionPlan`: the phase / trunk / fiber tree flattens CSR-style into plan-wide-sized flat arrays (a `phases` array, a flat `trunks` pool, a flat `fibers` pool), each phase and trunk carrying an offset-and-count range into the next pool. The `PlanDims` dimensions size the flat pools, collapsing the dense per-aggregate nesting that measured 11 MB at the default budget. Unifying these flat columns onto the scheduler's columnar store is the next step in the unified-storage arc.

Each step names its foundations crate dependency: `arvo-bitmask` for set ops, `arvo-graph` for DAG / RCM, `arvo-sparse` for CSR DependencyGraph (bench-locked canonical at all N), `arvo-spectral` for the Fiedler partition step, `arvo-comb` for the cost DP.

### Dispatch codegen

Sealed `trait DispatchCodegen<Cfg: RunCfg>` with TAIT `type CoreDispatch = impl Fn(...)`. Single shipped impl `StandardCodegen` lowers to closed-form arithmetic, zero `blr`. Three `DispatchApproach` variants ship in v1: ScheduleMega, TrunkMega, IndirectPerFiber.

Per-fiber-shape generic monomorphisation: `fn run_fiber<S: FiberShape>(...)` over sealed `FiberShape: Sealed` marker carrying `type WuTuple` + `const SHAPE_ID`. Combinatorics K (unique shapes), not 2^N. Sealed-trait dispatch is empirically free vs hand-written direct calls per the `dispatch_static` bench at every N.

### Adapt subsystem

Nine-axis `AdaptConfig` (not opaque modes): `pass_duration`, `phase_ema`, `fiber_ema`, `change_class`, `cache_residency`, `throughput_trending`, `predictive_parking`, `memory_watermark`, `core_idle_time`. Cadence-interleave via per-axis `sample_skip`. Convenience presets `off()/fast()/standard()/aggressive()` as const fn over the multi-axis space.

`AdaptMetrics` hot-cold split: scratch-arena hot path + `Resource<AdaptMetrics>` snapshot at ScheduleEnd. Per-fiber 64B-aligned hot lines (progress + inline metrics, AoS-packed within line); per-core 64B-aligned park slots; cold SoA arena for end-of-pass derived metrics. Per-axis sub-resources shipped individually (`PassDurationMetrics`, `PhaseEMAMetrics`, etc.); optional `MetricsKit` recipe bundles them.

Per-anomaly `Virtual` marker types (`BalanceAnomaly`, `MemoryPressure`, `ThroughputAnomaly`, `FiberEMADrift`, `PhaseEMADrift`) for observer composition via AccessSet union.

EMA vectorisation is cfg-gated `core::arch::*` SIMD from day one (NEON aarch64 + SSE2 x86_64 baseline + scalar fallback; AVX2/AVX-512/SVE expand bench-driven). NEON wins 4-9x over autovec at N≥1024 per the `ema_axis_h` bench.

### Thread pool + Executor

Open `trait Executor: Sealed` monomorphised. Default `HybridExecutor` impl. `Scheduler<'frame, Cfg, E = HybridExecutor>` propagation.

Three-tier parking via per-OS atomic-wait primitives: Linux `futex(2)`, macOS `__ulock_*`, Windows `WaitOnAddress`. Tier selection driven by per-phase `predicted_wait_ns` atomic slot in `PoolFrame`: <2µs spin-only, 2-50µs spin+futex, >50µs skip spin and park immediately.

`PoolFrame<MAX_CORES, MAX_PHASES>` engine-internal data plane on per-pool cache lines: `shutdown: AtomicBool`, `phase_arrived: AtomicU32`, `predicted_wait_ns: [AtomicU32; MAX_PHASES]`, `progress_slots: [AtomicUsize; MAX_CORES]`. Phase barrier = centralised atomic counter + futex-wake-all by last arriver.

Per-OS P/E core classification ships in v1: CPUID 0x1A (x86_64), `/sys/.../cluster_id` + cpufreq (Linux), `sysctl hw.perflevel0` (macOS), `GetSystemCpuSetInformation` (Windows). `WakeStrategy { p_spin_iters, e_spin_iters, futex_threshold_ns, park_threshold_ns }` per-CoreClass. Defaults P=128, E=32.

Work-stealing BACKLOG. Spin-budget calibration micro-bench at `ThreadPool::build()` BACKLOG; fixed 128 in v1. `Scheduler::Drop` sets `shutdown` flag, futex-wakes all parked, and waits on the exit counter until every worker has left its mainloop; the engine holds no thread handle to join, because the threads are the consumer executor's.

### Morsel loop

Forward iter by default; Topic 3 axis E adds head+tail convergence variant for commutative single-trunk-phase fibers. Morsel size baked into ExecutionPlan at plan-stage step 9 from SIMD-width + L1 write budget. Micro-morsel boundary every 64 records; sync via `core::hint::spin_loop` budget + 32-record drift tolerance (no futex inside morsel loop; phase barrier handles cross-phase parking).

Codegen emits `dmb st` (aarch64) / `sfence` (x86_64) between final FiberOutput non-temporal `stnp` writes and progress-counter Release store, per Topic 3 invariant S3.

Per-morsel vs per-record WU dispatch inferred from AccessSet shape: column-shaped WUs receive a `Morsel<T>` window; resource-only WUs dispatch per-record.

### Resource resolution

`ResourceSnapshot<'phase, R>` read-only typed view captured at a phase boundary; lifetime bounded to the current phase (no lift-out). Concrete signature lands with the data plane (HILA-RUNTIME-C6), reconciled against the current `BuilderInput` contract.

`PlanAffecting: BuilderInput + Sealed` marker trait. Default NOT plan-affecting. Consumers opt in for resources whose value shape changes the plan (e.g., `RunCfg` itself). `MAX_PLAN_AFFECTING_RESOURCES = 16` default cap; consumers override the const generic on Scheduler.

Persistence integration is consumer-supplied via `PersistenceProvider` trait + Provider tuple. The engine never depends on `hilavitkutin-persistence` types directly. PassEnd WU drains accumulator columns into persistence via `PersistenceProvider::commit`.

### Plan caching

`DependencyGraph` CSR-backed (bench-locked canonical at every N; the predicted N=16 crossover was wrong per the `dep_graph_csr_9` bench). Three columnar arrays at the engine level: `row_offsets: [USize; MAX_UNITS + 1]`, `col_indices: [UnitId; MAX_EDGES]`, `edge_kinds: [EdgeKind; MAX_EDGES]`.

Per-`PlanAffecting`-resource dirty bit array. Plan cache lives with Scheduler for the pipeline's lifetime: the cached plan is the flat `ExecutionPlan`, reused across frames and recomputed only when a `PlanAffecting` resource dirties. Sequential single-threaded recompute in v1 (parallelisation deferred until consumers ship 256+ WU pipelines). Moving the flat plan columns onto the scheduler's columnar store is the next step in the unified-storage arc.

### `WorkUnit::Ctx` GAT

`trait WorkUnit { type Ctx<'frame>; fn execute<'frame>(&self, ctx: &Self::Ctx<'frame>); ... }`. The GAT pattern keeps consumer `impl WorkUnit` blocks `<>`-clean (no `'frame` lifetime parameter required) while allowing the engine to thread `Pin<&'frame PoolFrame>` and related borrows through Ctx at dispatch time. Validated by the lifetime sketch under `mock/research/sketches/202605101036-poolframe-lifetime/`.

## The loop core

The contracts come first, in new crates beside the engine, with a one-thread reference runner under them that is kept as the oracle. Later fusion and threading land in the plumbing and demand no change of an author or an implementor; what the engine above describes stays as it is until it is ported onto the contracts. What follows holds for the five loop-core crates; the statements of the sections above that it departs from keep governing the legacy crates.

A kernel is a value kernel: it has no receiver and no context, is handed the values of its declared reads at one record and returns the values of its writes, and is handed no window, position or range. It carries no hint and no commutativity constant. A kernel declares a `KernelId` and a binding declares a `UnitId`, because a kernel is bound by data and so one kernel type is many units, and an order key needs an identity that is the same across builds.

A column a kernel names is a role, bound to a stored column by a binding, which is data; a per-binding coefficient is a resource value supplied by the binding and read as a `Param` member. `bind` checks the bindings once against the kernel list and the store's formats, refuses two writers of one stored column as `TwoWritersOfOneColumn` rather than ordering them, and makes the checked table `Bound`, from which alone the typed column handle `Col` is minted. The 128-bit word that refuses two kernels of one list with one id is a stand-in for arvo's masks, its width no interface.

A step reads a committed view and writes staged columns; a swap commits them. The reference runner's `run` is a library function over a checked table, a store and a visiting order, and a frame's digest is what every other runner is compared by. The laws a run is held to are functions over plain data, each with a mutant it must fail. This round does not classify an emitted kind's log or identity as a store kind; the code already holds a fourth store marker, `Accum<T>`, that the three store types of `hilavitkutin-api` do not list, and that drift is the lead designer's to resolve.

## Vocabulary

Authoritative hierarchy (coarsest → finest):

```
pipeline → core → phase ↔ waist → trunk → fiber ↔ branch ↔ bridge → morsel → micro-morsel → record
```

Loop-core words: binding (a line of the content table binding a kernel to stored columns), role (a column a kernel names, filled by a binding), logged form, member (of a read list), and `Lane` for the arithmetic vocabulary a kernel generic over a number is bounded by, as distinct from a column.

Dead terms (do not use): chain → fiber, chain group → trunk, partition → phase, archetype → fiber, entity → record, row → record, entry → record, order (on WU) → scheduling hints. See consolidation topic §Vocabulary for full table + rationale.

